      "range": [
        87,
        90
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " Broadly, UTF-8 encodes `char`s as a \"leading\" byte which begins the `char`,",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        91,
        170
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " followed by some number (possibly 0) of continuation bytes.",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        171,
        234
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        235,
        238
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " The leading byte can have a number of bit-patterns (with the specific",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        239,
        312
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " pattern indicating how many continuation bytes follow), but the continuation",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        313,
        393
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " bytes are always in the format `0b10XX_XXXX` (where the `X`s can take any",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        394,
        471
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " value). That is, the most significant bit is set, and the second most",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        472,
        545
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " significant bit is unset.",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        546,
        575
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        576,
        579
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " To count the number of characters, we can just count the number of bytes in",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        580,
        659
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " the string which are not continuation bytes, which can be done many bytes at",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        660,
        740
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " a time fairly easily.",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        741,
        766
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        767,
        770
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " Note: Because the term \"leading byte\" can sometimes be ambiguous (for",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        771,
        844
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " example, it could also refer to the first byte of a slice), we'll often use",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        845,
        924
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " the term \"non-continuation byte\" to refer to these bytes in the code.",
      "path": "doc",
